      "content": "Apologies for the confusion earlier. Let's dive into the science of your levain's microbial community, shall we? When the temperature in your kitchen rises, it's like throwing a wild party for your microbial guests, and the Lactobacillus and yeast are the main attendees. These little organisms have their own preferences and tolerances.\n\nLactobacillus, the sourdough bacteria, loves to produce lactic acid, which gives your levain its tangy flavor. But when the temperature spikes, these bacteria can become more active, and their lactic acid production can increase. This can lower the pH of your levain, making it more acidic. On the other hand, yeast, the leavening agent, prefers a warmer environment to produce carbon dioxide, which helps your dough rise. However, if the temperature gets too hot, the yeast can become stressed and produce more acetic acid, which can also lower the pH and affect the flavor of your bread.\n\nSo, when the temperature in your kitchen rises, it's like throwing a wild party for your microbial guests, and the Lactobacillus and yeast are the main attendees. These little organisms have their own preferences and tolerances. Lactobacillus, the sourd",
